\description{
Finds the number of clusters, the number of individuals per cluster, or both, for a two-condition cluster
randomized design (CRD), so that the confidence interval for the standardized difference between the condition
means has a desired width or is as narrow as a budget allows. Four of the functions fix one of the two sizes and
take a desired width or a budget; the two \code{both} functions take a budget and return the sizes that give the
narrowest interval, or take a desired width and return the least costly sizes. Each returns a table with the size
or sizes found, the cost, and the expected width of the interval where it was computed.

The interval is the likelihood-based confidence interval (Cheung, 2009) computed by the \code{OpenMx} package
(Boker et al., 2011). See Pornprasertmanit and Schneider (2014) for the method.
}
\details{
Here are the functions' descriptions:
\describe{
  \item{\code{ss_aipe_crd_es_n_clusters_fixed_width}}{Find the number of clusters given a specified width of the confidence interval and the cluster size}
  \item{\code{ss_aipe_crd_es_n_individuals_fixed_width}}{Find the cluster size given a specified width of the confidence interval and the number of clusters}
  \item{\code{ss_aipe_crd_es_n_clusters_fixed_budget}}{Find the number of clusters given a budget and the cluster size}
  \item{\code{ss_aipe_crd_es_n_individuals_fixed_budget}}{Find the cluster size given a budget and the number of clusters}
  \item{\code{ss_aipe_crd_es_both_fixed_budget}}{Find the sample size combinations (the number of clusters and that cluster size) providing the narrowest confidence interval given the fixed budget}
  \item{\code{ss_aipe_crd_es_both_fixed_width}}{Find the sample size combinations (the number of clusters and that cluster size) providing the lowest cost given the specified width of the confidence interval}
}

Every answer that targets a width, and the expected width a budget planner reports when
\code{G} and the population values are supplied, rests on an a priori Monte Carlo
simulation: a candidate design is evaluated by generating \code{G} data sets and reading
the likelihood-based confidence interval on the standardized effect size from OpenMx. The
planners whose answer needs one such evaluation, or a handful, run in about a second at a
small \code{G} and are the ones shown in the examples. The two that search over the
number of clusters, \code{ss_aipe_crd_es_n_clusters_fixed_width} at a fixed cluster size and
\code{ss_aipe_crd_es_both_fixed_width}, which repeats that search across candidate cluster
sizes and keeps the least costly combination that reaches the width, evaluate many
candidates in turn and run for minutes at the default \code{G = 1000}, so they are not
among the examples. A call such as
\code{ss_aipe_crd_es_n_clusters_fixed_width(width = 0.3, cluster_size = 20, true_es = 0.5,
es_type = 1, true_icc = 0.25, pr_treat = 0.5, G = 1000, seed = 113)} returns the number of
clusters of 20 individuals that brings the expected width of the interval on the
individual-level standardized effect size to 0.3, and
\code{ss_aipe_crd_es_both_fixed_width(width = 0.5, cluster_cost = 5, individual_cost = 1, true_es = 0.5,
es_type = 1, true_icc = 0.25, pr_treat = 0.5, G = 1000, seed = 113)} returns the least
costly pairing of clusters and cluster size at those costs that reaches a width of 0.5.
Both accept \code{diff_size} for unequal cluster sizes, in which case the simulated data
sets carry the unequal sizes and the confidence interval comes from a multiple-group model.

The width of a simulated interval is its upper limit minus its lower limit.
}
\section{Simulated data sets without a usable interval}{

Each simulated data set is fitted with \pkg{OpenMx}, and its interval is used only when
the fit and the search for each limit succeeded: the optimizer status code of the fit and
of each limit's search is 0 or 1 (the codes that \pkg{OpenMx}'s \code{as.statusCode()}
describes as \code{"OK"} and \code{"OK/green"}, optimization succeeded; with a code such
as 5, a Hessian that does not appear to be convex, or 6, a solution that does not meet
the first-order optimality conditions, the fit may not be at the maximum the interval is
measured from, and that help page advises against trusting it), \pkg{OpenMx}'s
diagnostic for each limit is \code{"success"}, and the two limits are finite and bracket
the estimate. A data set that fails any of these, or whose fit stops with an error,
gives no usable interval. How often that happens depends on the design. With
\pkg{OpenMx} 2.22.11 and a standardized effect of 0.5 (the intraclass correlation in
parentheses), the share of 1000 simulated data sets without a usable interval, with its
Monte Carlo standard error, was 0 with 20 clusters of 10 (0.2) and \code{es_type = 1},
0.002 (0.0014) with six clusters of 5 (0.1), 0 with ten clusters of 2 (0.01), 0 with
eight clusters of 4 (0.1) and \code{es_type = 0}, 0.415 (0.016) with six clusters of two
to four individuals (0.05), and 0.208 (0.013) with 20 clusters of 10 (0.2) and
\code{es_type = 2}; a second run of 400 data sets gave 0.378 (0.024) and 0.237 (0.021)
for the last two. These are single runs, and other versions of \pkg{OpenMx} can give
other shares. With the unequal clusters 412 of the 415 failed fits ended with status 5
or 6, and in 200 further data sets each of the 86 such fits had estimated the
between-cluster variance below 0. The effect of \code{es_type = 2} is
divided by the between-cluster standard deviation, which can be near 0: for some data
sets the likelihood does not fall far enough on one side for a finite limit to exist,
for others \pkg{OpenMx}'s search does not reach a limit that exists, and some of the
intervals it does find are very wide (with 20 clusters of 10, the usable intervals of
300 data sets had a median width of 2.1, 10 of 242 were wider than 10, and 2 wider than
100), so that their mean rests on a few data sets.

Without \code{assurance}, the expected width is the mean width over the data sets that
gave a usable interval, so it describes studies in which the interval could be formed.
With \code{assurance}, a data set without a usable interval counts as one whose interval
is wider than any width, and the assured width is the \code{assurance} quantile of the
widths of all \code{G} data sets; the assurance is then the probability that a study
gives an interval and that the interval is no wider than the assured width. When more
than about \code{1 - assurance} of the data sets give no usable interval, no width is
reached with that probability.

When some of the data sets simulated at the design reported gave no usable interval, a
warning of class \code{dmar_replications_without_interval} (with \code{scope}
\code{"design"}) gives their number, the reasons, and the number of data sets the width
is taken over. When every data set at the design reported gave an interval but some at
other designs the search simulated did not, a warning of the same class (with
\code{scope} \code{"search"}) gives the totals of the search, since those data sets bear
on where it stopped. When none gave one, or the assurance cannot be reached, the
function stops with an error of class \code{dmar_computation_failed} that carries the
counts (\code{attempted}, \code{returned}, \code{failed}, \code{failure_reasons}), and no
table is returned. Inside a search such a design counts as one whose width does not meet
the target. For a fit that stopped with an error, the warning and the error give the
first error message, which names the cause when every data set fails for one reason.

A warning that a fit gives is not discarded: each kind of warning is given once when the
function exits, as a warning of class \code{dmar_result_qualified} that says how many
simulated data sets gave it and carries its own class as \code{warning_class}. Warnings
of one class whose messages differ only in their numbers count as one kind; the first
message is shown, and all of them are in the field \code{messages}. The warning
\pkg{OpenMx} gives for an optimizer status other than 0 is not passed on: a fit of status
1 is used, as \pkg{OpenMx}'s help describes it, and any other status is counted as the
reason the data set has no usable interval.
}

\section{The searches}{

The planners that search for a size start from the size the unstandardized difference
between the condition means needs (the cluster size search with \code{es_type = 1} can
start lower; see below), and each size they try is a simulation of \code{G} data sets.
A width meets the target when it is no larger than the target.
From a start whose width meets the target, the search steps down one cluster
or one individual at a time to the smallest size that still meets it. From a start
whose width is above the target, the search steps up 1, 3, 7, 15, and so on, past the
start until the width meets the target, and then halves the gap between the last size
that did not meet it and the first that did until the two are adjacent. When the
simulated width falls as the size grows, this gives the smallest size that meets the
target, the size that stepping up one at a time gives, with far fewer
sizes simulated. With a small \code{G} the simulated width can rise and fall from one
size to the next, and then the two can stop at different sizes near the target. The
same holds when designs without a finite width (see below) lie among designs with one,
as can happen near the share of failed data sets an assurance allows: the search
returns a size that meets the target next to one that does not, which need not be the
smallest size that meets it.

The search for the number of clusters has no upper bound, since the width falls toward 0
as clusters are added wherever it is finite. A design at which no simulated data set
gives a usable interval, or at which too few do for the assurance, has no finite width;
after such a design the next size tried is at least twice as large, and three such
designs in a row stop the search with an error of class \code{dmar_computation_failed},
with \code{reason} \code{"no_finite_width"}, that gives the sizes, the counts, and the
reasons.

With a fixed number of clusters the width levels off as the clusters grow, because the
clusters themselves vary, so a target can be out of reach at any cluster size. For
\code{es_type = 1} with no covariate at the cluster level, the value it levels off at is
known: as every cluster grows, the within-cluster standard deviation becomes known and
the cluster means exact, and the likelihood-based interval for the difference between
\emph{K} cluster means with a common unknown variance has a width whose mean (or, with
\code{assurance}, whose quantile) has a closed form. A target at or below that value is
refused before anything is simulated, with an error of class
\code{dmar_argument_invalid}, \code{reason} \code{"width_not_reachable"}, that gives it
as \code{width_limit}; a target above it is reached by some cluster size. There the
search starts from the smaller of two sizes: the size the unstandardized difference
needs, and the size at which the expected width (or its quantile) with the
within-cluster standard deviation known falls below the target. That width is the value
the width levels off at, with the variance of a cluster mean at the size in place of the
between-cluster variance. The unstandardized difference uses the \emph{t} quantile on
\emph{K} - 2 degrees of freedom, and with few clusters it needs far more: with 10
clusters and an intraclass correlation of .2 it needs 1,875 individuals per cluster for
a target of 1.46, where about 10 reach it with \code{true_es = 0.5} (the size the
unstandardized difference needs does not depend on the effect, but the size that
reaches the target grows with it). With equal cluster sizes, no covariate, and no
\code{assurance}, the start is a lower bound on the cluster size the target needs (the
second of the two sizes uses \code{true_R2_within} only with a covariate, since the
simulated data use it only then).
The likelihood-based interval contains the interval computed with the within-cluster
standard deviation fixed at its maximum likelihood estimate, whose expected width is the
width with the deviation known times the mean of the ratio of the deviation to its
estimate, which exceeds 1; so at every smaller cluster size the expected width is above
the target. With \code{es_type = 0} or \code{2}, or with a covariate at the cluster level,
the search starts from the size the unstandardized difference needs, and only when that
is at most 100,000 individuals per cluster. The cluster size search simulates cluster
sizes up to 100 above the size the unstandardized difference needs (above its own start
when that size is beyond 100,000) and never above 500, since the model has one variable
per individual of a cluster and a fit takes far longer as the clusters grow; a search
that would start above 500 individuals per cluster is refused before anything is
simulated, with an error of class \code{dmar_argument_invalid}, \code{reason}
\code{"cluster_size_beyond_reach"}, that gives the start as \code{start} and, as
\code{lower_bound}, whether it is a lower bound in the sense just given; the message
calls it one only then. A width still above the target there stops it with an
error of class \code{dmar_computation_failed}, \code{reason} \code{"width_not_reached"},
that gives the sizes searched and the narrowest width found with its Monte Carlo
interval. The message says whether a larger cluster size reaches the target (where the
value the width levels off at is known) or that this was not determined, and suggests a
larger \code{G} only when the target lies within that Monte Carlo interval.

The number of clusters the unstandardized difference needs, from which the search over the number of
clusters starts, is at least \code{ceiling(2 / min(pr_treat, 1 - pr_treat)) + n_covariates}, which puts two
clusters in each condition (see \code{\link{ss_aipe_crd}}), and the search steps down no further than the
last design with two clusters in each condition. A start beyond 1,000,000 clusters, the most the unstandardized search
considers, is refused before anything is simulated, with an error of class \code{dmar_argument_invalid},
\code{reason} \code{"n_clusters_beyond_reach"}. The planners that search over both sizes start from the plan of the unstandardized difference,
which keeps the same floor, and do not simulate a number of clusters at which the budget does not buy
clusters of 2.

A call simulates at most 500 designs; the two planners that search over both sizes share
that bound among the searches they run, and three neighboring designs with no finite
width stop \code{ss_aipe_crd_es_both_fixed_budget}. A call that reaches the bound stops
with an error of class \code{dmar_computation_failed}, \code{reason}
\code{"search_budget"}, that gives the totals. These bounds limit the computation; none
of them is a statistical rule.
}

\section{Refused calls}{

The design arguments, the costs, and the budget are checked before anything is simulated, as in
\code{\link{ss_aipe_crd}} (see its section on refused calls): \code{n_covariates} a whole number of 0 or
more (and here at most 1), \code{cluster_size} a whole number of at least 2, \code{n_clusters} a whole
number of at least \code{3 + n_covariates} that puts a cluster in each condition, \code{pr_treat} strictly
between 0 and 1, \code{true_R2_between} and \code{true_R2_within} in [0, 1), \code{true_R2_between} 0
when \code{n_covariates = 0} (\code{reason} \code{"R2_between_without_covariates"}), \code{diff_size} \code{NULL} or a
vector of one or more finite numbers, and each cost and the budget a single finite number of 0 or more.
Wherever a width is simulated (always in the four planners that take a width
or search over both sizes, and with \code{G} in the other two), \code{es_type} must be 0, 1, or 2,
\code{true_icc} as described under its argument, and, with a covariate, \code{true_icc_covariate} in [0, 1].
Costs that are both 0 are refused where they choose the design
(\code{reason} \code{"costs_all_zero"}), and \code{individual_cost = 0} where they choose the cluster size
(\code{reason} \code{"individual_cost_zero"}). A budget that does not buy the smallest design a planner
can return is refused with \code{reason} \code{"budget_infeasible"} and the cost of that design as
\code{min_cost} (with \code{G}, the smallest design with a cluster in each condition and a degree of
freedom), and a budget whose design leaves a condition without a cluster, when its width is asked
for, with \code{reason} \code{"arm_without_clusters"}. A fixed \code{n_clusters} that leaves a condition
without a cluster is refused with the same \code{reason} where a width is simulated: always in
\code{ss_aipe_crd_es_n_individuals_fixed_width}, and in \code{ss_aipe_crd_es_n_individuals_fixed_budget}
only with \code{G}, since without it \code{pr_treat} is not used. Each of these has class
\code{dmar_argument_invalid}. \code{assurance} must be \code{NULL} or a single number strictly between 0
and 1 in all six planners (class \code{dmar_assurance_invalid}), and the three planners that take a width
refuse one that is not a single positive number. \code{ss_aipe_crd_es_n_individuals_fixed_width} refuses, with \code{reason}
\code{"cluster_size_beyond_reach"} and \code{start = Inf}, a search whose start, the cluster size the
unstandardized difference needs, is beyond 100,000.
}

\examples{
# Two of these planners answer a question the budget alone settles. Given
# what it costs to open a cluster and what it costs to collect one more
# individual, the first reports how many clusters a budget buys at a fixed
# cluster size and the second reports how large each cluster can be at a
# fixed number of clusters. Clusters cost nothing to open here and each
# individual costs 1, so the budget buys 1000 individuals and the only
# question is how to arrange them.
ss_aipe_crd_es_n_clusters_fixed_budget(budget = 1000, cluster_size = 20,
  cluster_cost = 0, individual_cost = 1)

ss_aipe_crd_es_n_individuals_fixed_budget(budget = 1000, n_clusters = 200,
  cluster_cost = 0, individual_cost = 1)

# The interval width these planners can report, and every answer that
# targets a width, rests on an a priori Monte Carlo simulation: a candidate
# design is evaluated by generating G data sets and reading the
# likelihood-based confidence interval on the standardized effect size from
# OpenMx. The calls below use G = 2 so that the page runs quickly; a
# reported plan deserves the default G = 1000. Each call describes a
# population standardized effect size of 0.5, with es_type = 1 putting that
# effect size in individual-level standard deviation units and a quarter of
# the outcome variance lying between clusters.

# Supplying G and the population values to a budget planner adds the
# expected width of the interval the affordable design buys.
ss_aipe_crd_es_n_clusters_fixed_budget(budget = 1000, cluster_size = 20,
  cluster_cost = 0, individual_cost = 1, true_es = 0.5, es_type = 1, true_icc = 0.25,
  pr_treat = 0.5, G = 2, seed = 113)

# Cluster size needed for a target width, given the number of clusters.
# With 250 clusters the planner settles on the smallest cluster size it
# will consider, two individuals per cluster, and the expected width still
# comes in under the target: for a contrast between conditions that are
# assigned at the cluster level, precision is bought with clusters rather
# than with what happens inside them.
ss_aipe_crd_es_n_individuals_fixed_width(width = 0.5, n_clusters = 250,
  true_es = 0.5, es_type = 1, true_icc = 0.25, pr_treat = 0.5, G = 2,
  seed = 113)

# Once recruiting a cluster costs 5, the number of clusters and the cluster
# size trade off against each other, and this planner searches the
# combinations the budget allows for the narrowest expected interval.
ss_aipe_crd_es_both_fixed_budget(budget = 1000, cluster_cost = 5,
  individual_cost = 1, true_es = 0.5, es_type = 1, true_icc = 0.25, pr_treat = 0.5,
  G = 2, seed = 113)

# Unequal cluster sizes. Every planner accepts diff_size, which gives each
# cluster's deviation from cluster_size as an integer offset or as a
# multiplicative factor, recycled across the clusters; the planner prints
# the resulting cluster sizes and their frequencies ahead of its table.
# The budget planner shows both forms here.
ss_aipe_crd_es_n_clusters_fixed_budget(budget = 1000, cluster_size = 20,
  cluster_cost = 0, individual_cost = 1, diff_size = c(-2, 1, 0, 2, -1, 3, -3, 0, 0))

ss_aipe_crd_es_n_clusters_fixed_budget(budget = 1000, cluster_size = 20,
  cluster_cost = 0, individual_cost = 1,
  diff_size = c(0.6, 1.2, 0.8, 1.4, 1, 1, 1.1, 0.9))

}
